const topics=[['lcm','LCM','Bell tower','When do two patterns meet?'],['hcf','HCF','Sharing camp','Make equal packs. Leave nothing behind.'],['fractions','Fractions','Pizza farm','Build with equal pieces.'],['mixed','Mixed numbers','Builder’s bridge','Connect wholes and fractions.'],['decimals','Decimals','Gem mine','Meet tenths and hundredths.'],['discounts','Discounts','Village market','Find a percentage. Work out the saving.'],['simple','Simple interest','Savings hut','Grow savings by the same amount.'],['compound','Compound interest','Growth garden','Let your savings grow on savings.']];
const lessons={
lcm:[['When do bells meet again?','One bell rings every 4 minutes. Another rings every 6. They both ring now. When will they next ring together?',['Count in 4s: 4, 8, 12. These are multiples of 4.','Count in 6s: 6, 12. These are multiples of 6.','The first positive shared number is 12. That is the least common multiple (LCM).'],'Zero is the start. LCM asks for the first meeting AFTER zero.'],['Find the first shared multiple','A bus arrives every 3 minutes and a train every 5. Both arrive now.',['Bus: 3, 6, 9, 12, 15.','Train: 5, 10, 15.','They meet again after 15 minutes. LCM(3, 5) = 15.'],'Multiplying the two numbers gives a common multiple, but it is not always the LEAST. For 4 and 6, 24 is common; 12 is first.'],['Use LCM for equal-size pieces','To add 1/4 + 1/6, both pieces need the same size.',['LCM(4, 6) = 12, so use twelfths.','1/4 = 3/12 and 1/6 = 2/12.','3/12 + 2/12 = 5/12.'],'LCM is useful for repeating events and finding a common denominator.']],
hcf:[['Make equal gift packs','You have 12 red chocolates and 18 yellow chocolates. Make as many identical packs as possible, using everything.',['A factor divides a number exactly: 12 ÷ 3 = 4, so 3 is a factor of 12.','Factors of 12: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12. Factors of 18: 1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 18.','The highest common factor (HCF) is 6. Make 6 packs, each with 2 red and 3 yellow chocolates.'],'HCF is the largest number that divides BOTH totals with no remainder. It is also called GCD.'],['Why not more packs?','Try making 4 identical packs from 12 red and 18 yellow chocolates.',['12 ÷ 4 = 3 red per pack. That works.','18 ÷ 4 = 4 remainder 2. That leaves yellow chocolates over.','Both totals must divide exactly. Six packs works for both.'],'LCM: first shared multiple. HCF: biggest shared factor.'],['Use HCF to simplify','A pizza has 12 equal slices. You eat 8. You ate 8/12 of the pizza.',['HCF(8, 12) = 4.','Divide the top and bottom by 4: 8 ÷ 4 = 2; 12 ÷ 4 = 3.','8/12 = 2/3. The amount of pizza has not changed.'],'To keep a fraction equal, divide its numerator AND denominator by the same nonzero number.']],
fractions:[['Equal pieces of one whole','Cut one pizza into 4 equal pieces. Take 3. You have 3/4 of the pizza.',['The bottom number, denominator, says how many equal pieces make the whole.','The top number, numerator, says how many of those pieces you have.','1/2 = 2/4 = 3/6. More cuts can make smaller pieces without changing the amount.'],'The pieces must be EQUAL. Bigger denominators mean smaller pieces of the SAME whole.'],['Add, subtract and compare','You eat 1/2 of a pizza, then another 1/4. How much did you eat?',['Change the half into quarters: 1/2 = 2/4.','Add the counts: 2/4 + 1/4 = 3/4. Do not add denominators.','For 5/6 − 2/6, subtract the counts: 3/6 = 1/2. To compare 3/4 and 2/3, use 9/12 and 8/12: 3/4 is bigger.'],'Use a common denominator so you count pieces of the same size.'],['Find a fraction of an amount','What is 3/4 of 20 apples?',['First find one quarter: 20 ÷ 4 = 5 apples.','Then take three quarters: 5 × 3 = 15 apples.','So 3/4 of 20 = 15. “Of” tells us to multiply: 20 × 3/4.'],'Divide by the bottom to find one part. Multiply by the top to find the parts you need.']],
mixed:[['Wholes plus a little extra','29 + 1/5 means 29 whole blocks and one fifth of another block.',['Write it as the mixed number 29 1/5.','Each whole has 5 fifths. So 29 wholes have 29 × 5 = 145 fifths.','Add the extra fifth: 145 + 1 = 146. So 29 1/5 = 146/5.'],'A mixed number contains a whole number and a fraction. An improper fraction has a top number at least as large as the bottom.'],['Build the conversion yourself','Convert 7 2/3 into an improper fraction, then a decimal.',['Seven wholes contain 7 × 3 = 21 thirds.','Add the 2 extra thirds: (7 × 3 + 2)/3 = 23/3.','23 ÷ 3 = 7.6666… exactly as a repeating decimal. Rounded to two decimal places, it is approximately 7.67.'],'Whole × denominator + numerator gives the new numerator. The denominator stays the same because the piece size stays the same.'],['Add and subtract mixed numbers','Build 6 3/4 blocks and then 2 1/4 more.',['Add the wholes: 6 + 2 = 8. Add the fractions: 3/4 + 1/4 = 1.','8 + 1 = 9 whole blocks.','For 10 2/3 − 4 1/3: subtract wholes and thirds to get 6 1/3. If needed, trade one whole for fractional pieces before subtracting.'],'To go backwards, divide: 23 ÷ 3 = 7 remainder 2, so 23/3 = 7 2/3.']],
decimals:[['A different way to write parts','One rupee is 100 paise. So 25 paise is 25/100 of a rupee, or ₹0.25.',['The first digit after the point counts tenths. The second counts hundredths.','0.3 = 3/10 = 30/100 = 0.30. Trailing zeros do not change the value.','0.25 = 25/100 = 1/4. Divide the numerator by the denominator to turn a fraction into a decimal.'],'0.5 is bigger than 0.25 because 50 hundredths is bigger than 25 hundredths.'],['Calculate with place value','You buy a pencil for ₹2.50 and an eraser for ₹1.25.',['Line up decimal points so rupees meet rupees and hundredths meet hundredths.','2.50 + 1.25 = 3.75.','If you pay ₹5.00, the change is 5.00 − 3.75 = ₹1.25.'],'Keep place values lined up. You can add zeros: 2.5 = 2.50.'],['Repeating decimals and rounding','Share 2 pizzas equally among 3 people. Each gets 2/3.',['2 ÷ 3 = 0.6666… . The 6 repeats forever.','To round to two decimal places, look at the third digit. It is 6.','Since it is 5 or more, round the second digit up: 0.67. So 7 2/3 ≈ 7.67, not exactly 7.67.'],'Keep exact fractions while calculating. Round only when the question asks.']],
discounts:[['Percent means out of 100','A shop gives 25% off a ₹200 backpack. What does that mean?',['25% means 25 out of every 100. It is 25/100 = 1/4.','Find one quarter of ₹200: 200 ÷ 4 = ₹50.','The discount is ₹50. You pay ₹200 − ₹50 = ₹150.'],'A discount is the amount saved. The sale price is the amount you pay.'],['Find any percentage','A ₹300 game is discounted by 15%.',['1% of ₹300 is 300 ÷ 100 = ₹3.','15% is 15 × ₹3 = ₹45 saved.','Sale price = ₹300 − ₹45 = ₹255.'],'Discount = original price × rate ÷ 100. Sale price = original price − discount.'],['Check a tempting offer','A ₹100 item gets 10% off, then another 10% off the reduced price.',['First discount: ₹10. New price: ₹90.','Second discount: 10% of ₹90 = ₹9.','Final price: ₹81. Total saving: ₹19, or 19%, not 20%.'],'Always ask: percentage of WHICH amount? A later discount acts on the new price.']],
simple:[['Same extra money each year','Imagine ₹1,000 in a savings box earning 10% simple interest per year.',['The starting money is the principal: ₹1,000.','10% of ₹1,000 = ₹100. This same ₹100 is added each year.','After 3 years, interest is ₹300. The total is ₹1,300.'],'With SIMPLE interest, the extra money is always calculated from the original principal.'],['Build the formula','A ₹500 deposit earns 8% simple interest per year for 2 years.',['For one year: ₹500 × 8 ÷ 100 = ₹40.','For two years: ₹40 × 2 = ₹80 interest.','Total = ₹500 + ₹80 = ₹580.'],'Interest = principal × annual rate × years ÷ 100. Total = principal + interest.'],['Time must match the rate','₹1,000 earns 10% simple interest per year for 6 months.',['Six months is half a year: 6/12 = 0.5.','One full year earns ₹100. Half a year earns ₹50.','Total after 6 months = ₹1,050.'],'When the rate is per YEAR, convert months into years by dividing by 12.']],
compound:[['The extra money grows too','Imagine ₹1,000 earning 10% compound interest, added once each year.',['Year 1: 10% of ₹1,000 = ₹100. New total: ₹1,100.','Year 2: 10% of ₹1,100 = ₹110. New total: ₹1,210.','The second year earns ₹10 more because last year’s ₹100 earns interest too.'],'COMPOUND interest uses the growing balance. SIMPLE interest uses the original principal.'],['Grow it one year at a time','Start with ₹200 at 10% interest compounded annually for 3 years.',['Year 1: ₹200 + ₹20 = ₹220.','Year 2: ₹220 + ₹22 = ₹242.','Year 3: ₹242 + ₹24.20 = ₹266.20. Interest earned = ₹266.20 − ₹200 = ₹66.20.'],'Total after n whole years = principal × (1 + annual rate/100)ⁿ. The power n means multiply by that growth factor n times.'],['Compare the two gardens','Put ₹1,000 at 10% per year in each imaginary savings box for 2 years.',['Simple: add ₹100 each year → ₹1,200 total.','Compound annually: add ₹100, then ₹110 → ₹1,210 total.','Compound gives ₹10 more here. Both earned ₹100 in the first year.'],'Our examples assume a fixed annual rate, annual compounding and no deposits, withdrawals, tax or fees.']]
};
const questions=[];
function q(topic,prompt,answer,others,hint,steps){questions.push({id:questions.length+1,topic,prompt,answer,options:[answer,...others],hint,steps})}
